WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:09.630
Competition breeds rivalry sometimes, it's between organizations. Their

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.300
legacies evolving with every exchange.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:22.620
(Casting)

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:27.500
Sometimes it's two competitors at the peak of their powers trading blows.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:45.880
(Casting)

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.820
But sometimes a rivalry is built from something else.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.660
A difference in mindset.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.900
A casual born from a lack of respect.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:01:00.060
When Huk and Idra in the early 2010s, it was the freshed face new hope,

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.340
against the grizzled, foul-mouthed, brood war veteran.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.020
And it was always worth watching.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:12.400
(Casting)

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:18.100
Hey, but before we get into it, if you want this sweet kappa shirt from our "What is" series,

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.480
or plenty of other theScore esports branded shirts just like it, you can check out theScore esports merch

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.060
department.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.040
Which you can find a link to in the comments below.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.380
"Ladies and gentlement let's introduce our next player, known as the Grakken.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.000
From Team Evil Geniuses, Idra."

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.520
Greg "Idra" Fields had long been a foreigner in a game dominated by Koreans.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.640
He even moved to the country and joined Korean teams like

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.420
East row and CJ entus. By the time Starcraft II rolled around Idra was the

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:57.360
foremost foreigner in Brood War. But Idra became known for something else too

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.840
being unafraid to speak his mind, shit talking on ladder and generally pushing

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.520
the limits of sportsmanship.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:13.200
(Stream)

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.800
"Alright, what do you think about cruncher and how do you think this match is gonna go."

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.600
"I'm playing really bad but it's more than enough to beat him."

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.160
"Alright guys, shake hands if you're going to."

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.600
(Casting)

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.920
As competition in the west mostly shifted towards Starcraft II, a new breed of foreign hopes

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:37.520
arose and one among them, Chris "Huk" Loranger showed promise.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.400
"This week you are actually in the finals so how do you feel about that?"

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.440
"I feel great I mean this is my second LAN of all time so the experience is

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.600
definitely adding up, ESL was a real challenge for me just

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.240
because it was my first LAN hopefully I continue to do well."

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:56.360
He boasted a strong win-loss record during the games beta period which earned him tournament

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.680
invites and a chance to play for Team Millenium."

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:03.680
"I do want to continue to emphasize that fact about Huk the fact that he's just laid back and he's very

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.120
embracing of all the situations he's in he doesn't even think of himself as like

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.690
the best player in the world he's always asking players for advice and when he

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.930
loses games and we've even seen Idra lose games and Idra tends to say oh

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:18.620
that's a terrible strategy you'll never get far doing that. But Huk says god I

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:21.470
can't believe you beat me that badly I gotta figure out how to not make that

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:24.800
happen. And he literally says it like that I've seen him do it in private

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.720
practice games with other players." His style was aggressive and unpredictable

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.240
switching between standard builds and more unpredictable strategies like the

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:33.500
mothership rush.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:04:02.560
(Casting)

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.160
But while he was hyped during the beta Huk fell short at his first post release

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.480
LAN in Cologne, exiting the tournament in the group stage.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.240
He bounced back quickly taking first place at MLG Raleigh 2010.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:25.600
(Casting)

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.160
The grizzled veteran and the rising star. Idra and Huk were on a collision

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.160
course. The two faced off at MLG DC 2010.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:58.040
(Casting)

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:02.200
Idra sent Huk, who had recently joined Team Liquid, to the losers bracket.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:06.840
With a 2-0. There against SeleCT, Huk tried out one of the tricks he was becoming known for,

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:07.840
the mothership rush.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.520
But it didn't work out. And Idra took the whole tournament for Evil Geniuses.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:19.120
"Uh so Huk, re*ard or poster boy for Starcraft II esports."

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.160
"Re*ard but since the community's re*arded he's the poster boy."

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:27.400
"Huk made the... He brought in the audience, they laughed it was great, but he threw the game away."

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.840
Even though the mothership failed and Idra had won the tournament.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:35.640
The move brought attention to Huk, and earned him fans in the nascent StarCraft II scene.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.600
Huk lost to Idra again at EG Masters Cup Series 3

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.840
With Idra displaying some serious BM as he attacked with his drones, and started building a hatchery

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.000
in Huk's main base.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:56.040
(Casting)

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.880
Idra lived in Korea at the time, and travelled to attend foreign events.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.000
While Huk was based in North America.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.760
But Huk made the move to Korea.

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:07.080
Beginning a series of encounters between the two players on ladder,

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:08.960
and further pushing the rivalry.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:27.120
(Casting)

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.000
Huk continued to use unconventional strategies, like cannon rush cheese against

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.280
Idra on ladder.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:36.720
Idra for his part, made it clear he had a distaste for Protoss players.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.960
"Nah he's a piece of trash who wouldn't play any games if he didn't play Protoss."

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:52.840
And their next LAN meeting, MLG Dallas 2011 featured one of the most infamous moments in their rivalry

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.760
Facing off in the group stage Idra took game 1 against Huk and it

00:06:56.770 --> 00:07:01.479
looked like he would have game 2 as well, he built up a considerable lead and

00:07:01.479 --> 00:07:06.250
was just one good engagement away from a win but Idra's late game army had one

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:10.330
major weakness, it wasn't terribly effective against air units like void

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:13.680
rays which Idra suddenly found himself facing down.

00:07:13.680 --> 00:08:16.960
(Casting)

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:20.320
The unit's might not have been real, but Huk's victory was.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:22.600
And Huk wanted Idra to know it.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.280
So he told him the next game.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.720
And got a predictable response.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:45.560
(Casting)

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.800
While Huk won the battle taking that series, Idra won the war.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.840
After the group stage they faced off again, with Idra winning 4-2.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.800
And after the event, he couldn't pass up the chance to talk more shit about Huk.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:58.560
"So Huk can't win in a real game huh?"

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:02.760
"Pretty much, he wouldn't win at all if he didn't play Protoss, he's actually kinda re*arded."

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:04.280
"Stand by your guns on that one."

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:07.200
"How good did it feel to knock him off after the games yesterday."

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:08.640
"Really really really good."

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:13.080
"I mean Idra can say what he wants, he likes to talk and lie a lot.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:17.920
I remember in a previous interview he said he beats me all the time when realistically the last 40 games we've

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:24.280
played he's won maybe five. Just sometimes a cocky f*ck and all this other sh*t so uh, you know

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:29.760
I tried being nice to Idra, Idra wants to continue doing whatever he's doing, it's fine with me I don't care."

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.480
And while Huk did eventually get the better of Idra,

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:37.120
Wins at Home Story Cup 3, and the North American Blizzard Invitational, put Huk on the board.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:40.320
The latter victory denying his opponent a spot at Blizzcon.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:50.440
(Casting)

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:53.880
And by August, the rivalry took an even stranger turn.

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.760
Huk left Team Liquid to join Evil Geniuses,

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.640
turning him and Idra from rivals to teammates.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:03.880
"They played it up in the promotional stuff about you vs Huk and this big rivalry thing.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:11.040
How much was it like a rivarly of dislike before for you and Huk when he was in other teams."

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.680
"We never disliked each other personally we always got along pretty well, we have really similar senses of

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:15.560
humour actually."

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:20.920
"We're pretty good friends even before he joined EG. Ah but I just didn't respect his play very much,

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:25.560
and kinda said so, but he's improved a lot, obviously he's been practicing in Korea for a long time.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.040
so there's no friction at all there anymore."

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:34.520
Unable to weaponize your fanbases the same way they used to, that was pretty much the end of Huk and Idra's

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:35.040
rivalry.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:37.600
Idra continued to talk sh*t of course.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:40.680
He just directed it at other players after he joined EG.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:44.000
And after eventually quitting StarCraft II in mid 2013,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:49.520
Idra had a very brief stint in Heroes of the Storm. Representing EG in the games very first Blizzcon

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:50.120
appearance.

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:53.320
"Honestly a lot calmer playing Heroes of the Storm.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:56.560
The biggest thing is it's a team game, and it's purely a team game.

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:00.920
It's the opposite end of the spectrum from Brood War, all that matters is how well you work with your team.

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:03.720
And the strategy thinking portion of the game.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:08.760
And if you get pissed off and start raging then that starts affecting your teammates.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:14.140
Recently Idra revealed he hasn't owned a computer in some time. And these days he's mostly focused on

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:14.640
fitness.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:17.600
"No I haven't owned a functioning desktop in like two years now.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:21.760
I was just a really competitive person and StarCraft happened to be the competition that stuck.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:25.880
And now... I don't know I was kind of an angry competitor so I don't know how healthy that is.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:29.840
So I don't compete anymore, I think weightlifting kind of filled that role for me.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:36.280
But no StarCraft was just a way to beat people."

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:39.400
Huk meanwhile has successfully transitioned into Overwatch.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:44.120
Where he started as a caster, and has since become the president of gaming for the Kraft group.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:45.720
Which owns the Boston Uprising.

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:48.360
One player has left esports behind,

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:50.920
and the other is helping build a new pro scene.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:55.280
But for just under two years, while the western StarCraft II fanbase was looking for

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:56.600
heroes and villains,

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.960
Idra and Huk gave them exactly what they needed.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:03.040
One a foul mouthed confident veteran,

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:08.840
"You're kind of notorious for being a little bit bad mannered, and me knowing you personally I would say

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:10.760
say that might be based in some truth."

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:12.400
"What were your thoughts on that game."

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:13.800
"I just played really sh*tty."

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:15.840
"Short and sweet."

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:18.080
The other a fresh faced new hope,

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.040
who knew how to play the crowd.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:28.160
(Casting)

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:35.880
"Just thank you, I think I have the best fans in the world, and I love you guys and I'm glad you enjoyed my games."

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.560
And despite their rivalry being short lived,

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:42.360
the intensity of the two players and the passion of their fans,

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:45.160
made their conflict one to remember.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:13:01.560
(Casting)

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:08.800
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

